### KVM allows QEMU to use the host hardware-accelerated virtualization capability. The host CPU needs to support it and it needs to be enabled in BIOS.
 QEMU can still launch VMs without KVM, but things will be slow without hardware acceleration. 
 
 In this scenario, we are using nested virtualization because srv-01 is an Ubuntu VM running on an ESXi 5.1 host. We need to enable nested hardware virtualization support in ESXi for the guest OS during build
 so that srv-01 can use KVM.

 hardware virtualization support for a guest OS on an ESXi host is enabled by checking the "hardware virt support - Expose hardware assisted Virtualization to the guest OS" option under "Customize Hardware->CPU"
 in the web vCenter client during VM build. This option is not available in the regular vCenter client and is greyed out if the ESXi host CPU does not
 support CPU hardware virtualization. 
 
  The ESXi host that srv-01 runs on supports hardware acceleration for guest OS because this option in the web client is not greyed out for the VM. This means that we can use KVM on srv-01 ###
 
